---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { frontendSeries } from '../../data/series';
const inlineStyle = `
/* ── Hero ── */
.fe17-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#264de4 0%,#2965f1 40%,#00b894 100%)}

/* ── CSS evolution ladder ── */
.css-art{margin:2.5rem auto 0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem}
.css-rung{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.72rem;padding:0.35rem 1.1rem;border-radius:0.5rem;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.12);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);border:0.0625rem solid rgba(255,255,255,0.2);animation:cssRise 0.4s ease both}
.css-rung:nth-child(1){animation-delay:0.05s}
.css-rung:nth-child(2){animation-delay:0.18s}
.css-rung:nth-child(3){animation-delay:0.31s}
.css-rung:nth-child(4){animation-delay:0.44s;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.22);font-weight:700}
@keyframes cssRise{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(8px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}

.fe17-num{color:#264de4;background:#eef2ff}

/* ── Box model diagram ── */
.box-model{display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;margin:1.25rem auto;max-width:22rem;gap:0}
.bm-layer{width:100%;padding:0.6rem 0;text-align:center;font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:600;border-radius:0.5rem;margin-bottom:0.25rem}
.bm-margin{background:#fdf2ef;color:#e17055;border:0.125rem dashed #e17055}
.bm-border{background:#f0efff;color:#6c5ce7;border:0.125rem solid #6c5ce7}
.bm-padding{background:#e8f8f5;color:#00b894;border:0.125rem solid #00b894}
.bm-content{background:#e3f2fd;color:#0984e3;border:0.125rem solid #0984e3;padding:1rem 0}

/* ── Specificity table ── */
.spec-table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:1.25rem;font-size:0.88rem}
.spec-table th{background:#eef2ff;color:#264de4;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;text-align:left;border-bottom:0.125rem solid #ddd}
.spec-table td{padding:0.55rem 0.9rem;border-bottom:0.0625rem solid #eee;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.82rem}
.spec-table tr:nth-child(even) td{background:#fafafa}
.spec-win{color:#00b894;font-weight:700}
.spec-lose{color:#d63031}

/* ── Global scope demo ── */
.chaos-demo{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem}
@media(max-width:600px){.chaos-demo{grid-template-columns:1fr}}

/* ── SCSS before/after ── */
.scss-compare{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem}
@media(max-width:600px){.scss-compare{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
.scss-col-label{font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.05em;padding:0.25rem 0.6rem;border-radius:0.3rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem;display:inline-block}
.scss-bad{background:#fdf2ef;color:#e17055}
.scss-good{background:#e8f8f5;color:#00b894}

/* ── Quiz theming ep17 ── */
.quiz-ep17f .quiz-q-num{color:#264de4;background:#eef2ff}
.quiz-ep17f .quiz-reveal-btn{color:#264de4}
.quiz-ep17f .quiz-opt-letter{color:#264de4}
.quiz-ep17f .quiz-option:hover{border-color:#264de4;background:#eef2ff}
.quiz-ep17f .quiz-answer-highlight{background:#eef2ff;border-left:0.25rem solid #264de4}
`;
const bodyHtml = `

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero fe17-hero">
  <div class="hero-badge">Episode 17 of 23</div>
  <h1>CSS —<br>The Language of Style</h1>
  <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.95)">HTML gives a page structure. CSS gives it appearance. Invented in 1996 to separate content from presentation — but plain CSS had problems that took 25 years of tooling to solve.</p>
  <div class="css-art">
    <div class="css-rung">1996 — CSS1: colors, fonts, margins</div>
    <div class="css-rung">1998 — CSS2: layout, positioning, floats</div>
    <div class="css-rung">2006 — SCSS/Sass: variables, nesting, mixins</div>
    <div class="css-rung">2017+ — CSS3 modules: flexbox, grid, custom properties</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">01</span>
  <h2>Before CSS: Presentation Mixed Into HTML</h2>
  <p>When the web started, there was no CSS. Developers who wanted visual design had to put presentation directly into HTML tags:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">&lt;!-- Early web: presentation tangled into structure --&gt;</span>
&lt;<span class="code-keyword">font</span> color=<span class="code-string">"red"</span> size=<span class="code-string">"3"</span>&gt;Important text&lt;/<span class="code-keyword">font</span>&gt;
&lt;<span class="code-keyword">table</span> bgcolor=<span class="code-string">"#000000"</span> border=<span class="code-string">"1"</span>&gt;...&lt;/<span class="code-keyword">table</span>&gt;
&lt;<span class="code-keyword">b</span>&gt;&lt;<span class="code-keyword">i</span>&gt;Bold italic&lt;/<span class="code-keyword">i</span>&gt;&lt;/<span class="code-keyword">b</span>&gt;</pre>
  </div>

  <p>The problems were obvious: to change a colour across a 50-page site, you had to edit every single HTML file. Structure and design were impossible to maintain separately.</p>
  <p>In 1994, <span class="highlight">Håkon Wium Lie</span> — working at CERN alongside Tim Berners-Lee — proposed CSS. The idea: put all visual rules in a separate file, link it to HTML, and let the browser combine them. <span class="highlight">CSS 1 became a W3C standard in December 1996.</span></p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">02</span>
  <h2>What CSS Does: Selectors + Properties</h2>
  <p>CSS is a collection of rules. Each rule has a <span class="highlight">selector</span> (what to target) and <span class="highlight">declarations</span> (what to change):</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">/* selector  { property: value; } */</span>

<span class="code-keyword">h1</span> {
  color: <span class="code-string">#264de4</span>;
  font-size: <span class="code-string">2rem</span>;
  margin-bottom: <span class="code-string">1rem</span>;
}

<span class="code-keyword">.card</span> {           <span class="code-comment">/* class selector */</span>
  background: <span class="code-string">#fff</span>;
  border-radius: <span class="code-string">0.5rem</span>;
  padding: <span class="code-string">1.5rem</span>;
}

<span class="code-keyword">#submit-btn</span> {     <span class="code-comment">/* ID selector */</span>
  background: <span class="code-string">#0984e3</span>;
  color: <span class="code-string">#fff</span>;
}</pre>
  </div>

  <p>The browser reads the HTML, builds the DOM (Episode 4), then applies all CSS rules to each node in the tree. The result is the rendered page.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">03</span>
  <h2>The Cascade: Which Rule Wins?</h2>
  <p>CSS stands for <span class="highlight">Cascading Style Sheets</span>. The cascade is the algorithm that decides which rule applies when multiple rules target the same element. The key concept is <span class="highlight">specificity</span>.</p>

  <table class="spec-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Selector type</th><th>Example</th><th>Specificity</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Inline style</td><td><code>style="color:red"</code></td><td class="spec-win">highest</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ID selector</td><td><code>#submit-btn</code></td><td class="spec-win">high</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Class selector</td><td><code>.card</code></td><td>medium</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Element selector</td><td><code>h1</code>, <code>div</code></td><td class="spec-lose">lowest</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">If two rules have the same specificity, the one that appears <strong>later in the file wins</strong>. This is the cascade — rules flow down and later ones override earlier ones at equal specificity.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">/* Both target the same h1 — which wins? */</span>
<span class="code-keyword">h1</span> { color: red; }         <span class="code-comment">/* element selector — low */</span>
<span class="code-keyword">.title</span> { color: blue; }    <span class="code-comment">/* class selector — wins */</span></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">04</span>
  <h2>The Box Model</h2>
  <p>Every HTML element is a rectangular box. CSS's <span class="highlight">box model</span> describes the four layers that make up that box:</p>

  <div class="box-model">
    <div class="bm-layer bm-margin">margin — space outside the border (pushes other elements away)</div>
    <div class="bm-layer bm-border">border — the visible edge line</div>
    <div class="bm-layer bm-padding">padding — space between border and content</div>
    <div class="bm-layer bm-content">content — text, image, or child elements</div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">There is a famous gotcha: by default, when you set <code style="font-size:0.875rem">width: 200px</code>, that only counts the <em>content</em>. Padding and border are added on top — so the element ends up wider than 200px. This surprises almost every developer once.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">/* The fix — applied globally in almost every project */</span>
*, *::before, *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;  <span class="code-comment">/* width includes border + padding */</span>
}

<span class="code-comment">/* Now: width:200px means 200px total, always */</span></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">05</span>
  <h2>The Problem with Plain CSS at Scale</h2>
  <p>CSS works fine for small sites. But in a large app like dealmaker-ui with hundreds of components, plain CSS has serious problems:</p>

  <div class="chaos-demo">
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">Global scope</strong>
      <p style="font-size:0.88rem;margin-top:0.5rem;line-height:1.6">Every CSS rule applies everywhere. A <code>.button</code> class in one component file affects every element with <code>class="button"</code> across the entire app. One developer's change breaks another component.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">No variables (originally)</strong>
      <p style="font-size:0.88rem;margin-top:0.5rem;line-height:1.6">The brand primary colour appears in 50 files. When it changes, you update 50 files — and drift creeps in when different developers use slightly different hex values.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">No nesting</strong>
      <p style="font-size:0.88rem;margin-top:0.5rem;line-height:1.6">To style a button inside a card, you write <code>.card .button {...}</code> as a flat, separate rule. Deep relationships mean long repetitive selectors spread across the file.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">Repetition</strong>
      <p style="font-size:0.88rem;margin-top:0.5rem;line-height:1.6">The same set of rules (flex centre, border-radius, box-shadow) appears in dozens of places with no way to name and reuse them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">06</span>
  <h2>SCSS — CSS With Superpowers</h2>
  <p>In 2006, Hampton Catlin created <span class="highlight">Sass</span> (Syntactically Awesome Stylesheets), and the SCSS syntax followed (which looks like regular CSS with extras). SCSS compiles to plain CSS — the browser never sees SCSS directly. Vite handles the compilation. The dealmaker-ui repo uses <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.scss</code> files throughout.</p>

  <div class="scss-compare">
    <div>
      <span class="scss-col-label scss-bad">Plain CSS — repetitive</span>
      <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:0">
        <pre><span class="code-comment">/* No variables */</span>
.btn { background: <span class="code-string">#0984e3</span>; }
.link { color: <span class="code-string">#0984e3</span>; }
.border { border-color: <span class="code-string">#0984e3</span>; }

<span class="code-comment">/* Flat selectors */</span>
.card { ... }
.card .title { ... }
.card .title:hover { ... }</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="scss-col-label scss-good">SCSS — clean</span>
      <div class="code-block" style="margin-top:0">
        <pre><span class="code-comment">/* Variable */</span>
$primary: <span class="code-string">#0984e3</span>;
.btn { background: $primary; }
.link { color: $primary; }

<span class="code-comment">/* Nesting */</span>
.card {
  .title {
    &amp;:hover { ... }
  }
}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">SCSS also adds <code style="font-size:0.875rem">@mixin</code> (reusable rule groups you call like functions) and <code style="font-size:0.875rem">@use</code> / <code style="font-size:0.875rem">@import</code> for splitting styles into multiple files that compile into one CSS output.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe17-num">07</span>
  <h2>CSS Custom Properties — Native Variables</h2>
  <p>In 2015, CSS finally gained native variables called <span class="highlight">custom properties</span>. Unlike SCSS variables, these survive into the browser — you can read and change them with JavaScript at runtime.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">/* Defined on :root — available everywhere */</span>
:root {
  --primary: <span class="code-string">#0984e3</span>;
  --radius: <span class="code-string">0.5rem</span>;
}

<span class="code-comment">/* Used anywhere */</span>
.button {
  background: var(--primary);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
}</pre>
  </div>

  <p>SCSS variables are replaced at compile time and disappear from the output. CSS custom properties are actual browser features: they stay in the output CSS, are visible in DevTools, and can be updated with JavaScript — useful for dark mode or runtime theming.</p>
  <p>Both have their place: SCSS for nesting, mixins, and compile-time logic; custom properties for runtime values that need to change.</p>
</section>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#eef2ff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #264de4">
      <strong style="color:#264de4">CSS origin</strong> — Håkon Wium Lie, 1996. Separates presentation (CSS) from structure (HTML). One file styles an entire site.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">Cascade &amp; specificity</strong> — ID beats class beats element selector. Equal specificity: later rule wins. <code>box-sizing: border-box</code> fixes the width gotcha.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">Plain CSS problems at scale</strong> — global scope, no variables, no nesting, repetition. Fine for small sites, painful for large apps.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">SCSS</strong> — adds variables ($var), nesting, mixins. Compiles to plain CSS at build time. Used throughout dealmaker-ui.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">CSS custom properties</strong> — native browser variables (--primary: #blue). Survive to runtime, changeable via JavaScript. Different from SCSS variables which disappear at compile time.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKPOINT QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep17f">
  <span class="section-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What problem did CSS solve when it was introduced in 1996?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>It replaced JavaScript for making pages interactive</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>It allowed pages to load faster by removing images</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>It separated presentation from structure — styles moved out of HTML tags into a separate file</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>It let developers write styles using JavaScript syntax</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">&#10003; Correct: <strong>(C) It separated presentation from structure — styles moved out of HTML tags into a separate file</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Before CSS, visual styles were embedded directly in HTML: &lt;font color="red"&gt;, bgcolor attributes on tables, nested bold and italic tags. To change a colour across an entire site, you had to edit every HTML file. CSS moved all presentation into a single .css file that could be linked from any page — change the CSS file once and the whole site updates.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Two CSS rules both target the same element — <code>.card h2</code> and <code>h2</code>. Which one wins and why?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>The rule that appears first in the file</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>The shorter selector always wins</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>.card h2 wins — it includes a class selector which has higher specificity than a plain element selector</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>The last rule in the file always wins regardless of selector type</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">&#10003; Correct: <strong>(C) .card h2 wins — class selector has higher specificity than element selector</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">.card h2 combines a class (.card) with an element (h2). Class selectors have higher specificity than element selectors, so this combined rule beats a plain h2 rule regardless of file order. The cascade compares specificity first — only when specificity is exactly equal does file order matter (later wins).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What is the key difference between an SCSS variable ($primary) and a CSS custom property (--primary)?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>SCSS variables work in the browser; CSS custom properties only work during the build</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>They are the same thing — CSS custom properties are just newer syntax for SCSS variables</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>SCSS variables are replaced at compile time and disappear; CSS custom properties survive in the browser and can be changed with JavaScript at runtime</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>SCSS variables support more property types than CSS custom properties</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">&#10003; Correct: <strong>(C) SCSS variables replaced at compile time; CSS custom properties survive into the browser</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">When Vite compiles SCSS, $primary: #0984e3 gets substituted everywhere it appears — the output CSS contains literal hex values, no variable references. CSS custom properties (--primary: #0984e3) are actual browser features: they remain in the output CSS, are readable in DevTools, and can be updated with JavaScript at runtime — useful for dark mode or live theming without a rebuild.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEXT -->
<section class="next-part" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#264de4,#00b894)">
  <h2>Ready for Episode 18?</h2>
  <p>We know what CSS is. Now — what if you never wrote custom CSS at all? Tailwind takes a completely different approach: predefined utility classes applied directly in your HTML.</p>
  <a href="part18.html" class="btn btn-white">Continue to Episode 18 &#10132;</a>
</section>
`;
---
<ArticleLayout title="How the Frontend Works — Part 17: CSS — The Language of Style | Unbox the Black Box" inlineStyle={inlineStyle} series={frontendSeries} episodeNum={17} hasQuiz={true}>
<Fragment set:html={bodyHtml} />
</ArticleLayout>
